# PlatformIO build output, downloaded toolchains and library dependencies.
# ~40 MB and fully reproducible from platformio.ini.
.pio/

# Editor / IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.code-workspace

# Build artefacts, if ever produced outside .pio
*.bin
*.elf
*.map

# OS noise
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
# PyInstaller working directories -- the exe is the artefact, not these.
tools/build/
tools/__pycache__/
tools/*.spec

# Affinity Designer's lock file. Written beside a document while it is open, and it
# carries a session UUID and the name of whoever has it -- machine state, not work.
# Tracking one would conflict on every open and reveal nothing.
*~lock~

# The design document is ours, not the public repo's. It stays on disk.
#
# It is a directory now, not one file -- DESIGN.md is the index and the parts live under
# design/notes/. Both lines are needed: design/ IS tracked (the canopy drawings and HUD.af
# are build inputs), so the notes have to be excluded by name or they would ride in with it.
DESIGN.md
design/notes/
# THE REGRESSION HARNESS HAS TO SURVIVE, and it did not.
#
# Both the baseline and the session it was taken against were ignored, so the baseline
# ended up naming a .phr that no longer existed. A baseline whose session is gone cannot
# be compared with anything, which is the same as having none.
#
# So one canonical session is tracked, and the baseline with it. 431 KB and 9 hashes.
# Everything else under captures/ stays out: those are recordings of a moment, not a test.
captures/*
!captures/regress.phr
# AND THE SECOND CANONICAL SESSION, for the same reason and at a real cost: 2.8 MB on
# disk, 630 KB packed. It is kept because it is the only recording that contains an
# ANOMALY, and because regress.phr is 5,444 frames while phantom_regress samples out to
# 16,000 -- five of the nine sample points had never been reachable until this file.
#
# regress.phr stays. It is the short one, and a change that only needs a four minute
# check should not have to pay a sixteen minute render.
!captures/phantom-20260809-221925.phr
# The second tracked session, and for the same reason. regress.phr is a COURSE run: its
# fight is thin and its ordnance barely moves, so it cannot answer anything about combat.
# This one is 150 s of a deliberately missile-heavy match -- 8,442 frames, 6,264 distinct
# input structures, 73% of them under 59 fps -- and tools/ordnance-baseline.json is measured
# against it. 668 KB.
!captures/ordnance.phr

# Visual Studio working directory
.vs/
